          "event_id": "run-35082352371-review-lifecycle",
          "findings": [
            {
              "evidence": "Insert(T) passes the lazy GetBsonDocs iterator to the engine at lines 19 and 74-84, and enumerable Insert/InsertBulk/Upsert use the same lazy helper. LiteEngine.Insert and Upsert create a write snapshot with addIfNotExists=true before their foreach first advances that iterator (Engine/Engine/Insert.cs:20-30 and Upsert.cs:19-30). For a new collection, CollectionService.Add allocates the collection and primary-index pages before RemoveDocId can reject a get-only nullable Id or incompatible String/ObjectId member. Those page IDs enter TransactionPages.NewPages; the exception path calls TransactionService.Rollback, whose ReturnNewPages writes and confirms empty pages plus a changed header in the WAL (TransactionService.cs:295-299,331-399). The same occurs for a lazy/mixed batch when a valid prefix causes page allocation before a later item is rejected. Thus no document prefix is committed, but a failed call can still durably change file/allocation state, contrary to the required pre-engine validation and unchanged-byte atomicity. The frozen #2811/#2590 cases pre-create the target collection and put the invalid item first, so they do not exercise this boundary.",
              "path": "LiteDB/Client/Database/Collections/Insert.cs",
              "severity": "major",
              "summary": "Lazy preflight still permits durable allocator changes before rejecting an invalid model"
            },
            {
              "evidence": "The new single-item path invokes the user-controlled _id.Setter at line 83 while LiteEngine.AutoTransaction is active. If a custom non-null setter throws System.IO.IOException, AutoTransaction catches it and calls EngineState.Handle; EngineState.cs:41-48 classifies every IOException as an engine storage failure, calls LiteEngine.Close(ex), and returns false, so AutoTransaction skips its normal Rollback/ReleaseTransaction branch at Transaction.cs:97-102. The failed model assignment therefore shuts down the entire database session, and subsequent operations fail validation with the stored exception. This is newly reachable for Insert(T)/Upsert(T), whose setters previously ran after the engine call, and it violates the required error/disposal assessment for arbitrary throwing custom setters even though an ordinary InvalidOperationException takes the normal rollback path.",
              "path": "LiteDB/Client/Database/Collections/Insert.cs",
              "severity": "major",
              "summary": "A user setter IOException is treated as database I/O corruption and disposes the engine"
            }
          ],
